from ./Data import Deletion
from ./Email import Email

export anchor Access:
    model:
        A person may use the product with no account. Usage is counted
        against a free allowance for that browser or device. An account
        exists to carry the work and the allowance across devices, to
        subscribe, and to keep bought credits.
    fullyUseful:
        The free allowance must let a person finish a real naming job. It
        is a smaller quantity of the same product, never a crippled
        sample.
    signInRequired:
        Signing in is never required to see how the product works, to
        write a company description, or to see the first names. It becomes
        useful at the gate.

export anchor Limits:
    meaning:
        Every counted limit is a setting, not a fixed number in the
        specification. The operator sets the free allowance, what each plan
        gets, the size of a generation round, and what each effort level
        costs. None of these numbers is decided here.
    defaults:
        The free allowance ships set to 5 generation rounds of 12 names
        each, 3 revision rounds and 20 domain checks per month. These are
        starting values the operator can change.
    where:
        The settings are edited in the operator's admin interface, never
        in a source file and never by a customer.
    shape:
        A setting holds one number: a count of rounds, of names in a round,
        of checks, or a multiplier for an effort level. The set of settings
        is fixed by the specification. Only their values change.
    effect:
        A changed value applies to the next counted action. Rounds,
        revisions and checks already spent are never refunded and never
        taken away. An allowance already shown to a person keeps its
        meaning for the month in progress.
    record:
        Each change records the setting, the old value, the new value, the
        time and the operator who made it.
    fallback:
        If an admin value is missing or invalid, the last valid value stays
        in use and the person is never blocked by a bad setting.

export anchor FreeAllowance:
    what:
        A set of counters on a browser or device, or on the account when
        the person is signed in. Each counted action lowers a counter. The
        allowance refreshes every month, on the same day of the month as
        the first visit.
    amount:
        Set by the operator, with 5 generation rounds, 3 revision rounds
        and 20 domain checks as the shipped default. See {Limits}.
    counted:
        - Each generation round lowers the rounds counter by the level's multiplier, which is 1, 2 or 3
        - Each revision round lowers the revisions counter by one
        - Each domain check lowers the checks counter by one
    notCounted:
        - A name that cannot form a valid .com domain spends no check
        - Opening the site and reading the explanation
        - Writing a company description before a round starts
        - Comparing, renaming or removing names already on screen
        - Copying or downloading a shortlist already on screen
    fullJob:
        The shipped default of 5 generation rounds of 12 names each, 3
        revision rounds and 20 domain checks covers one complete naming job
        without payment. The operator may change the default, but a free
        allowance that no longer covers one complete job breaks the
        promise above. A person at the highest effort level may spend more
        of those counters per round, so a person who uses only the top
        level finishes fewer rounds in the same allowance.
    higherLevels:
        Higher effort levels are not reserved for paying people. A person
        with no account may spend allowance on any level, and a higher
        level spends more of it than a lower one. With the shipped
        multipliers the free allowance buys 5 rounds at Quick, 2 at
        Balanced, or 1 at Thorough. The cost of each level is shown before
        the round starts.
    visibility:
        The remaining allowance is visible at all times without opening a
        settings page. Before a round starts, the person sees what it will
        cost.

export anchor Gate:
    when:
        The gate applies when a counted action would take a counter below
        zero. Nothing already produced is removed. The list, the notes and
        the check results stay.
    shows:
        - What ran out, in plain words
        - When the allowance comes back, if it does
        - Sign in, to keep the work and use the account's own allowance
        - Subscribe, to raise the limit
        - Buy credits, to continue without a subscription
    blocked:
        Only new counted actions are blocked. Reading, comparing,
        removing, and copying or downloading what is already on screen
        keep working.
    timing:
        A person never sees a price, a sign-in form or a limit before they
        ask for something that costs allowance, and never in the middle of
        an edit they have not submitted.

export anchor Plans:
    pro:
        One person, one account. Pro includes every feature of the product
        that one person uses. It is billed per person.
    studio:
        The higher of the two plans, designed for a studio. Studio
        includes everything Pro includes and adds organization controls:
        one owner pays for every seat, members are added and removed by
        the owner, and projects can be shared across the organization. It
        needs at least three seats.
    seats:
        A seat is one person with their own sign-in. Studio bills per
        seat at a lower price than Pro. Buying fewer than three seats is
        not possible.
    discountWording:
        The Studio price is a little lower per seat than Pro, because a
        whole studio buys several seats at once. The lower price is volume
        pricing, not a lesser plan: what a plan includes, not its price,
        is what makes Studio the higher tier. The exact amounts are open
        questions. The lower price is described as a per-seat discount,
        never as free seats or a trial.

export anchor Billing:
    subscription:
        A monthly subscription in one of two tiers, Pro and Studio.
        Studio is the higher tier: it includes everything Pro includes and
        adds organization controls. A plan sets a monthly allowance of
        generation rounds, revision rounds and domain checks, and a level
        of access to the higher effort levels.
    topUps:
        Optional credit packs bought in addition to a subscription. They
        are never required to use the product.
    creditMeaning:
        One credit buys one generation round at the default effort level,
        Balanced. A round at a higher level spends more credits in the same
        ratio as the level's multiplier, so Thorough spends three credits
        and Quick spends one. A revision round and a domain check never
        spend credits, because they are covered by the plan's own counters.
    spendingOrder:
        The monthly allowance of the plan is spent first. Credits are
        spent only after it is used up.
    noCreditsLeft:
        A round that would cost more than the credits left is shown as
        unavailable rather than refused after it starts. Credits are only
        offered for sale when the plan's own allowance cannot cover the
        next round.
    creditRules:
        Credits belong to the account or the organization, not to a
        device. A person with no account cannot buy credits, because a
        purchase needs an account to attach to. Credits never expire. They
        stay usable after a subscription ends, and they go only when they
        are spent or when the account is deleted.
    creditAfterPlan:
        Ending a subscription turns off the plan's monthly allowance and
        its level of access to the higher effort levels. It never removes
        a credit already bought. How far a plan that has ended still covers
        revision rounds and domain checks, which the plan's counters
        normally pay for, is an open question.
    payment:
        The payment provider is Stripe. It holds the card and takes the
        payment, and card data never reaches the application. The
        application sees only whether a subscription is active, which plan
        it is on and how many people it covers, and never the card number.
    honesty:
        The price, the billing period and what each plan includes are
        shown before payment. A person is never charged while a free
        allowance is still available, unless they chose to buy.
    studioChanges:
        Removing a seat, or dropping below three seats, never deletes a
        project or a name. What happens to the price at that moment is an
        open question.

export anchor Accounts:
    anonymous:
        With no sign-in, the application keeps an anonymous identity in
        the browser and counts usage against it. No email address is asked
        for, and no password is created.
    signIn:
        Sign-in is offered, never required before the first name is
        generated. A person signs in to keep the work and use the account's
        own allowance across devices, to subscribe, to buy credits, or to
        join a Studio organization. Two methods are offered: an email
        address with a password, and an email address with a magic link,
        which is a single-use sign-in link sent by email with no password.
    carryOver:
        Signing in attaches the work to the account. Counters do not move:
        a signed-in person always counts against the account's own
        counters, on every device, and the device's counters stop counting
        from the next counted action.
    attached:
        Every project made on the device before sign-in is attached to the
        account, with its candidate list, rejected names, notes, domain
        check results and usage history. Nothing is dropped, and nothing is
        rewritten. If the account already holds a project of the same name,
        the person chooses which copy to keep.
    noSecondAllowance:
        Signing in must not hand out a second free allowance in the same
        month. At sign-in, the account's used count for the month in
        progress becomes the higher of the account's own count and the used
        count of the device it just attached. An account never ends up with
        more allowance than the larger of the two positions, and an account
        that already has a subscription is never moved down by attaching a
        device that used more.
    sharedCounters:
        An account's counters are one set for every device it signs in on.
        A second device never adds allowance, because both devices read and
        spend the same counters.
    signOut:
        Signing out returns the device to anonymous counting. The device
        does not win back allowance the account spent, and it starts at
        zero spent this month if it had not been used before.
    separation:
        Work attached to an account belongs to the account. Clearing
        browser storage after sign-in never deletes it, and deleting the
        account is what removes it.
    reset:
        Clearing browser storage starts a fresh anonymous count. A looser
        ceiling per network address stops that from being a way to get
        unlimited free use. See @{ResetDefences}.
    privacy:
        A company description is private by default. It is never shown to
        another person except through a shared project the person chose to
        put it in, never public, and never sold. The model provider must be
        used under terms that do not train on this data, and the provider
        terms are an open question.
    email:
        Sign-in links, invitations, receipts and admin notices are sent by
        email, and nothing else is. See @{Email}.
    deletion:
        A person can delete an account and its projects. Deletion removes
        the work, the usage record and any stored description. Deleting an
        organization does not delete the personal accounts of its members.
        The details are under @{Deletion}.

export anchor ResetDefences:
    counting:
        The browser or device counter is the one the person sees. A second,
        looser counter sits behind it, per network address, and is never
        shown as allowance.
    ceiling:
        The network ceiling allows several times the free allowance before
        it stops anything, because a home, an office and a school can share
        one address. The operator sets it in the admin interface, with a
        starting value of ten times the free rounds for one month.
    reached:
        When the ceiling is reached, the person sees a plain message: the
        free use from this network is used up for this month, and signing
        in or subscribing continues the work. Work already on screen stays
        on screen, and no name is removed.
    neverBlocks:
        The ceiling never blocks a signed-in account, a subscribed plan or
        a bought credit. It only limits free use with no account.
    sharedAddress:
        A person wrongly caught by a shared address is never left with
        nothing: signing in with an account that has allowance of its own
        always continues the work.
    addressPrivacy:
        A network address is stored only as a one-way hash, kept for the
        current month, and used for nothing else than counting and rate
        limits. It is never shown to another person, never joined to a
        name or a description, and never sold.
    honesty:
        Nothing in the product hides that a limit exists. The message says
        which limit was reached, and the ways to continue.
